WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.860
Logs are often copied, indexed, retained, searched, and shared with more systems than the original application data.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.280
That makes them a risky place for secrets.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:24.380
Passwords, access tokens, refresh tokens, API keys, private keys, session identifiers, one-time codes, password reset links, and raw authorization headers should generally not be logged.

4
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:33.200
If a value can be used to impersonate a user, call an API, decrypt data, or bypass a control, it does not belong in ordinary logs.

5
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.800
Sensitive personal data and payment data also require minimization.

6
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:52.700
Full payment card numbers, government identifiers, medical details, private messages, precise location data, and excessive request bodies can create privacy, regulatory, and breach impact problems if collected unnecessarily.

7
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.560
Sometimes an application needs to record a stable identifier, category, or outcome.

8
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.100
That does not mean it needs the full raw payload.

9
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:09.560
Prefer safe identifiers, redaction, hashing where appropriate, and purpose-specific fields over broad request logging.

10
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:19.040
Exception messages can accidentally contain secrets because libraries may include connection strings, query parameters, headers, file paths, or payload fragments.

11
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.180
Defensive logging treats exceptions as data to review and sanitize before exposure.

12
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.480
Avoid logging entire request bodies by default.

13
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:33.260
Avoid logging user-controlled text without length limits and encoding in log viewers.

14
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:41.840
The best secure logging design collects the minimum sensitive data needed for operations and investigation, then protects that data as if it will matter later.
